// =====================================================================
//
//        LINE MAZE SOLVER  -  MASTER CODE  (multi-algorithm)
//        Arduino Nano + ArduinoShieldV1 + 2x TB6612FNG + 5 IR sensors
//
// =====================================================================
//
//  ONE program, FIVE maze-solving algorithms. Choose one by changing
//  `algorithmMode` in SECTION 1 below - nothing else needs to change.
//
//  +------+----------------------------+-----------------------------------+
//  | mode | Algorithm                  | How it works                      |
//  +------+----------------------------+-----------------------------------+
//  |  0   | LEFT-HAND RULE             | Always prefer Left > Straight >   |
//  |      | (wall follower)            | Right. Records every decision,    |
//  |      |                            | then removes dead-end detours     |
//  |      |                            | ("path simplification").          |
//  +------+----------------------------+-----------------------------------+
//  |  1   | RIGHT-HAND RULE            | Same, but Right > Straight > Left.|
//  |      | (wall follower)            |                                   |
//  +------+----------------------------+-----------------------------------+
//  |  2   | DIJKSTRA                   | Builds a MAP (junctions + lines + |
//  |      | (map until END found)      | driving time) while exploring.    |
//  |      |                            | Never circles a loop. When END is |
//  |      |                            | found, Dijkstra computes the      |
//  |      |                            | fastest route over that map.      |
//  +------+----------------------------+-----------------------------------+
//  |  3   | TREMAUX FULL EXPLORATION   | Like mode 2, but does NOT stop at |
//  |      | + DIJKSTRA                 | END: it explores EVERY line of the|
//  |      |                            | maze first (depth-first, Tremaux).|
//  |      |                            | Slower dry run, but the final run |
//  |      |                            | is GUARANTEED the shortest route. |
//  +------+----------------------------+-----------------------------------+
//  |  4   | FULL EXPLORATION + A*      | Same full map as mode 3, planned  |
//  |      | (A-star search)            | with A*: Dijkstra + a distance-   |
//  |      |                            | to-END "hint". Same answer, fewer |
//  |      |                            | search steps (see Serial Monitor).|
//  +------+----------------------------+-----------------------------------+
//
//  Which one should I use?
//    - Wall followers (0, 1): simplest to understand. They can get stuck
//      circling a LOOP in mazes that have loops, and the final route is
//      not always the shortest.
//    - Dijkstra (2): fast dry run, loop-safe, shortest route over what
//      the robot has seen.
//    - Full exploration (3, 4): longest dry run, but the true shortest
//      route of the whole maze. The map uses driving TIME as distance,
//      so keep the speed steady (charged battery, same lfspeed) during
//      the dry run.
//
//  HOW TO RUN (all modes)
//    1. Press CALIB  -> bot spins and calibrates sensors, impeller starts
//    2. Press START  -> DRY RUN (explores the maze)
//                       Both LEDs blink when the dry run is finished.
//    3. Put the robot back on START.
//       Press START  -> FINAL RUN (follows the computed path)
//       Press START again to repeat the final run.
//       Press CALIB any time here to re-calibrate (the path is kept).
//    Open Serial Monitor (9600 baud) to see the map and the path.
//
//  PIN MAP (ArduinoShieldV1)
//    Motor A (LEFT)  : AIN1 = D4, AIN2 = D2, PWMA = D5
//    Motor B (RIGHT) : BIN1 = D7, BIN2 = D8, PWMB = D6
//    (STBY is tied HIGH on the shield)
//    CALIB button    : D13 (pressed = LOW)     START button : D11
//    LED5            : D12                     LED6         : D10
//    Impeller (ESC)  : D3                      Line sensors : A0..A4
//      A0 = far left, A1 = left, A2 = centre, A3 = right, A4 = far right
//
//  FILE LAYOUT
//    SECTION 1  : user settings  (algorithm, arena colour, speeds, tuning)
//    SECTION 2  : pins + motor driver
//    SECTION 3  : global variables
//    SECTION 4  : sensors + calibration
//    SECTION 5  : line following, PID, junction detection
//    SECTION 6  : movement (turns, brakes)
//    SECTION 7  : LEDs + buttons
//    SECTION 8  : ALGORITHMS 0 + 1   (wall followers + path simplification)
//    SECTION 9  : ALGORITHMS 2,3,4   (maze map + exploration)
//    SECTION 10 : PLANNERS           (Dijkstra and A*)
//    SECTION 11 : dry run, final run, setup(), loop()
// =====================================================================


